## Method and reasoning
Process rows in order. Keep bitmasks for occupied columns and for the two diagonals attacking the current row. Intersect their complement with the row’s allowed columns. Choose one available bit and shift the updated diagonal masks for the next row. A forced square is simply a row mask with one bit. Work depends on the number of legal partial placements, with O(n) depth.

## Walk through the sample
The forced queen in column 2 of row 1 rules out columns 1, 2 and 3 in row 2. Row 2 must use column 4. The remaining rows use 1 and 3, yielding exactly one arrangement.

## Complexity
For fixed-width masks, each edge of the search tree costs O(1). Time is proportional to the number of legal partial placements visited, at most O(n!) after reading the board. The row masks and recursion stack use O(n) space.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

long long countWays(const vector<unsigned> &allowed, unsigned full, int row, unsigned cols,
                    unsigned left, unsigned right) {
    if (row == int(allowed.size()))
        return 1;
    unsigned free = allowed[row] & ~(cols | left | right);
    long long total = 0;
    while (free) {
        unsigned bit = free & -free;
        free ^= bit;
        total += countWays(allowed, full, row + 1, cols | bit, ((left | bit) << 1) & full,
                           (right | bit) >> 1);
    }
    return total;
}
int main() {
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    unsigned full = (1u << n) - 1;
    vector<unsigned> allowed(n, full);
    for (int r = 0; r < n; ++r) {
        string s;
        cin >> s;
        size_t col = s.find('Q');
        if (col != string::npos)
            allowed[r] = 1u << col;
    }
    cout << countWays(allowed, full, 0, 0, 0, 0) << '\n';
}
